#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082941 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/id #uuid "e03fa018-0d27-5038-8c77-cf6bb723f9cf" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5676" :question/problem "Suppose a teacher needs to select a group of $4$ students from a class of $20$ students to represent the class in a competition. How many different ways can the teacher form a team of $4$ students?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}\n$$\n\nThere are $20$ students, so $n=20$. We want to select $4$ of those students to form a group, so we have $r=4$.\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways in which the teacher can form the team is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n_{20}C_{4} &= (20!)/((20 - 4)!4!) \\\\\n&= \\frac{20!}{16!4!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{20 \\cdot 19 \\cdot 18 \\cdot 17 \\cdot 16!}{16!4!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{116280}{24} \\\\\n&= 4845\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082942 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "120f626e-dd00-537a-8948-d8f81850d696" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group Fewer Than Ten"}] :question/id #uuid "e87f2616-514d-5b27-9f41-055c1f32f0cf" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5677" :question/problem "There are $10$ employees in a company, and $3$ of them are to be selected to form a committee. How many different selections are possible?" :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ employees, so $n=10$. We want to select $3$ of those employees to form a committee, so we have $r=3$.\n\nTherefore, the number of different ways to form the committee is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n_{n}C_{r} &= _{10}C_{3} \\\\\n&= (10!)/((10 - 3)!3!) \\\\\n&= \\frac{10!}{7!3!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{10 \\cdot 9 \\cdot 8 \\cdot 7!}{7!3!} \\\\\n&= \\frac{720}{6} \\\\\n&= 120\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082948 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "320acdc4-d3ca-5bc8-85de-cea9cc2fa9c3" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations"}] :question/id #uuid "2c61e1e6-58d0-56dd-a63d-29bd5239add1" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-5723" :question/problem "Evaluate ${}_{7}C_5$." :question/worked-solution "The number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!r!}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=7$ and $r=5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} {}_{7}C_5 &= \\dfrac {7!} {(7 - 5)!5!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {7!} {2!5!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {7 \\cdot 6 \\cdot \\cancel{5!}} {2!\\cancel{5!}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {7 \\cdot 6} {2 \\cdot 1} \\\\[5pt] & = \\dfrac {42}{2} \\\\[5pt] &= 21. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083025 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b6b720d4-59b0-5d86-b856-ff69abfe4152" :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating Combinations Given Using Alternate Notation"}] :question/id #uuid "bafe3204-ab4c-5db4-ba21-b979e3090cd5" :question/is-example true :question/math-academy-id "e-7458" :question/problem "Evaluate $(12; 10)$" :question/worked-solution "First, note that $\\displaystyle{{12}\\choose{10}}$ means the same thing as $_{12}C_{10}$.\n\nIn general, the number of combinations of $n$ objects taken $r$ at a time is given by the formula\n\n$$\n{}_nC_r = \\dfrac{n!}{(n-r)!\\,r!}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=12$ and $r=10$, we get\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel} \\begin{aligned} _{12}C_{10} &= \\dfrac {12!} {(12 - 10)!10!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {12!} {2!10!} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {12 \\cdot 11 \\cdot \\cancel{10!}} {2!\\cdot\\cancel{10!}} \\\\[5pt] &= \\dfrac {12 \\cdot 11} {2} \\\\[5pt] &= 66. \\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083485 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d22726fc-8e6b-5e22-80c8-fbc2aa245217" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Number of Combinations of Objects Chosen From a Group of at Least Ten"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9a21cebc-2f20-56dc-82b5-ab0ceb879864" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "148" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080241} {:answer/id #uuid "ca6ccb82-f120-5f5a-a6c3-02a5ec1cf9e3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "41144" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080242} {:answer/id #uuid "42aea4af-c9df-5857-8c52-053755965004" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "17" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080243} {:answer/id #uuid "49537f50-974d-5240-becb-e659a67a58bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080244} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1826b1-510b-547f-b6e7-3103a6992222" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "95040" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080245}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49537f50-974d-5240-becb-e659a67a58bb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "792" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080244} :answer-field/id #uuid "2d6dcfee-8b80-5e57-8e2e-4fe5219bfbfb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186080240}] :question/id #uuid "2d32c3c8-7746-5815-818e-26c7ca488adb" :question/is-example false :question/math-academy-id "q-1789" :question/problem "> A calculator is required to answer this question.\n\nA basketball coach must choose a starting lineup of five players from a roster of twelve players. How many potential starting lineups are there? Assume that the order in which the players are selected doesn't matter." :question/requires-calculator true}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083492 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point